WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
YURI GRINSHTEYN: In
our last episode,

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.960
we covered using distributed
tracing with OpenTelemetry

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.780
to identify the sources
of application latency.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.950
But what do you do once
you know which microservice

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.080
is causing the issue?

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.230
Or what if your service is
serving an elevated error rate?

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.580
What do you do then?

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.350
This is where logging comes in.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.850
Today, I'm going
to show you how you

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.210
can use Cloud Logging to ingest,
route, store, and view logs

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.820
from your services, and use
them to fully understand

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.260
what your service is doing.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.478
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:28.478 --> 00:00:31.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.840
But first, what do we
mean when we say logs?

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.270
We use logs to specifically
describe an event that

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:38.950
takes place in our system.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.260
Logs are written by our
code, by the platform

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.560
our code is running on, and the
infrastructure we depend on.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.880
Because logs and modern
systems are the descendants

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.320
of and sometimes still are
text log files written to disk,

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.960
a log entry analogous
to a line in log file

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.150
is the quantum unit of logging.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.880
An entry will generally
consist of a timestamp that

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.250
indicates either when
the event took place

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.020
or when it was ingested
into the logging

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.110
system, and the payload,
either as unstructured

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.000
text data or structured
data, most commonly in JSON.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.313
Logs can also carry
associated metadata,

00:01:11.313 --> 00:01:13.230
like the resource that's
writing them, the log

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.900
name, and the severity
for each entry.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.260
In Google Cloud, logs originate
from multiple sources.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.330
First, Google Cloud
Services admit

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.910
logs for both auditing and
troubleshooting purposes.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.700
These are enabled by default
and cannot be disabled directly.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.520
The infrastructure provision
and apps you run on Google Cloud

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.260
can also emit logs.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.660
These are largely controlled
by you, as the user.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.930
Finally, networking components
and services emit logs as well.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.690
Some of these,
like VPC flow logs,

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.240
can be controlled by the user,
while others are considered

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.480
part of the platform
and cannot be disabled.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.430
No matter where
the logs come from,

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.170
Cloud Logging
receives log entries

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.000
through the Cloud Logging API.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.820
From there, they pass
through the log router.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.490
The sinks in the log
router check each log entry

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.227
against existing inclusion
and exclusion filters

00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:01.810
that determine if
the log entry should

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.840
be sent to storage destinations,
including Cloud Logging

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.930
buckets, or excluded
entirely from ingestion.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.970
You can use sinks to route
logs to multiple destinations,

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.390
such as Google Cloud
Storage for archiving,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.550
BigQuery for
analytics, or Pub/Sub

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.280
to be processed further by
services such as Cloud Dataflow

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.890
or consumed by
third-party systems.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.630
This is also where
exclusions happen,

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.100
if you have any configured.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.760
Each sink lets you enter one or
more exclusion filters, which

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.190
let you exclude matching log
entries from being routed

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:31.780
to the sink's destination.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.040
Typically, these are used as
a cost optimization approach.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.290
Next, logs are sent
to log buckets,

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.990
which act as containers in
your Google Cloud projects

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.450
to store and organize
your logs data.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.250
The logs that you
store in Cloud Logging

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.380
are indexed, optimized,
and delivered

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.783
to let you analyze
your logs in real-time.

00:02:49.783 --> 00:02:51.700
Note that these are
different storage entities

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.980
than Cloud Storage buckets.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.590
You can create sinks to
route all or just a subset

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.750
of your logs to any log bucket.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.192
This flexibility allows you
to choose the Cloud project

00:03:01.192 --> 00:03:03.400
in which your logs are stored
and what other logs are

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.960
stored with them.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.660
Once your logs are
in buckets, you

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.880
can use log views to
control who has access

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.920
to the logs within
your log buckets.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.560
You can centralize or
subdivide your log storage

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.000
based on your needs.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.310
Custom log views provide
you with a granular way

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.010
to control access to the
logs in your buckets.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.290
For example, consider
a scenario in which

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.790
you store all of
your organization's

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.980
logs in the central project.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.250
Because log buckets can contain
logs from multiple projects,

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.920
you might want to control which
projects different users can

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.390
view logs from.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.060
Using custom log views, you
can give one user access

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.610
to logs only from a single
project, and another user

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.540
access to logs from
all the projects.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.570
So far, you've seen how platform
application and networking

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.570
logs are ingested through
the Cloud Logging API,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.270
and how the logs router sends
them to their destinations,

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.850
either in log buckets
or logs sinks.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.980
Next, let's have a look
at how you can view

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.780
and query logs in
Cloud Operations,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.560
starting with the Logs Explorer.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.880
To access the Logs Explorer,
go to the Products menu.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.180
From Logging, select
Logs Explorer.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.570
Initially, this shows you
all logs in your project.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.600
On the left side, you can
use Log Fields to select just

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.220
the logs that you care about.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.620
For example, if you have
a service running on GKE

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.110
and you want to figure out
what errors it's generating,

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.840
select Kubernetes
Container as the resource,

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.900
the name of your container,
and Error as the severity.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.600
This will filter all the
logs down to just error logs

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.640
being produced by your service.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.410
The histogram shows
you the volume

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.870
of logs that matches your
query over the selected time

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:39.810
interval.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.380
If you want to zero in on
a specific point in time,

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.600
just drag the selectors
to the time you care about

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.470
and rerun the query.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:49.420
Logs that match your
query are shown here,

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.310
in the Query Results section.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.830
To get the full details
of a log entry, select it.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.890
This will show you both the
metadata and the payload.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.870
To see other logs that
have the same payload

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.120
or any other field, click
a field and selection

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.670
Show Matching Entries to
rerun the query for those.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.455
The query is shown up here.

00:05:08.455 --> 00:05:09.830
You can edit it
directly, and you

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.510
can save it to be used later.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.490
Saved queries show up here.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.070
Logs Explorer even generates
suggested queries for you

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.240
based on what's happening
in your project.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.930
This is how you can use the Logs
Explorer to find the exact log

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:22.640
entries you need
to determine what's

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.450
happening with your service.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.130
The Logs Explorer
is great at helping

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:29.267
you find the logs that you
need when you need them.

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:30.850
But what if you're
debugging a service

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:33.230
and want to watch the
logs in real-time?

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:34.930
This is where logs
streaming comes in.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.040
Let's have a look.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:38.990
In order to stream
logs, you first

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:40.890
have to find the logs
that you care about.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.350
You can do that
by writing a query

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.150
or by using the log
field selectors.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.400
So let's say you want to
troubleshoot a Cloud Composer

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.360
workflow.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.860
Select Cloud
Composer Environment

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.060
on the left-hand side.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.180
Once you've run the query, you
have the option to stream logs.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.250
Click that button, and
the Query Results section

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.448
will expand to make it easier
to see and select log entries.

00:06:00.448 --> 00:06:02.990
The logs are now streamed, with
the newest entries showing up

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:04.055
at the top of the list.

00:06:04.055 --> 00:06:06.680
To pause the stream so that you
can inspect a particular entry,

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:08.680
just scroll down.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.455
Then, select an
entry, just as before.

00:06:11.455 --> 00:06:12.830
Once you're done
with that entry,

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.480
you can restart the
stream, and you'll

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.030
see the most recent logs at
the top of the list again.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.370
If you have the information
you need, stop the stream,

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:21.315
and get back to the query.

00:06:21.315 --> 00:06:22.940
So that's how you
can use log streaming

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.310
to see the log entries as
they appear, essentially

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.780
in real-time.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.940
Streaming logs on the
console is fantastic,

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.010
but what if you prefer
to use the command line?

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.540
Well, there's now
a GCloud command

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.200
you can use to do just that.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.350
Let's have a look at that.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.540
To stream logs from
the command line,

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:43.230
simply use the gcloud
alpha logging tail command.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.690
Use a filter to get just
the logs you care about.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.720
You can use the Query Editor
to get the filter correct.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.600
When you enter the command, the
tail session will be initiated,

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.080
and the logs will start
streaming into the console.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.957
As you can see, this
can be hard to read.

00:06:56.957 --> 00:06:59.040
So you may want to redirect
this to an output file

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.240
to make it easier.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.520
That's how you can use the
command line to stream logs

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:06.780
by using the gcloud alpha
logging tail command.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.290
Thanks for joining me today.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:11.160
We covered the basics of Cloud
Logging, how logs are ingested,

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.040
routed, and stored, how you
can query your logs from log

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.590
buckets using Logs Explorer,
and how you can stream them

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.570
in real-time using the
console and the command line.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:21.780
I hope this helps you find
issues faster and keep

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.360
your services reliable
and your users happy.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:25.660
Thanks so much for watching.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:27.827
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:07:27.827 --> 00:07:30.300
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.470
See you soon.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:34.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

